\section{Execution rules and inference contract}\label{app:implementation}\label{app:inference}
\subsection{Candidate functions and fixed continuation}
Visual reject runs the selected local head for the entire suffix. The other local options run one of three alternative heads or their four-head ensemble for 48 feedback steps, or hold the request/arrival selected-head action for 48 steps. Source options execute packet prefixes for 16/48 steps, suffixes beginning at index $d$ for 16/48 steps, or the equal blend of the current local action and the aligned source action for 48 steps. Every option returns to the same selected local feedback controller. Prefixes and suffixes are different executable hypotheses, not timestamp corrections chosen after testing.

The visual channel experiment changes only the packet supplied to these functions and the packet feature block. A refreshed packet generated at $a$ still uses the same request-delay term and suffix index $d+\text{elapsed}$; the receiver does not learn or receive a new generation-age feature. This intentionally narrow fixed-decoder intervention can discard the beginning of a fresh chunk. Its effect must not be described as optimal refresh value or as the performance of an age-aware controller. The missing symbol invokes the fixed reject wrapper without imputing numerical coordinates.

State local options comprise reject; two nonselected nearest-neighbor policies for eight feedback steps; request-action, arrival-action and zero-action holds for eight steps. Source options hold the one returned action for 1/8/32 steps or blend it equally with current local feedback for eight steps. All then return to the selected local controller. Shared noisy observations are time-indexed by native step. API observation containers retain only the declared ten coordinates; other entries are zero.

Local pose heads use additional offline pose targets converted through the public task controller. Visual critics use training-only block normalization (standard-deviation floor 0.05), median-positive-distance scaling and $B=(K+\lambda I)^{-1}Y$ with gain divided by ten. Bandwidths are 0.25/1/4 and ridge values 0.01/0.1/1/10. Candidates within $10^{-12}$ of global minimum validation MSE tie toward zero, then stronger ridge and smaller bandwidth. State critic targets are unscaled gains; their float64 networks use validation-only checkpoint selection. Detailed optimization, normalization exports and selected grids are in Online Resource 1.

\subsection{Primary uncertainty and reproducibility}\label{app:reproduce}
\input{source_data/inference_table}
The original visual confirmation additionally allocated six one-sided exact positive-event bounds. With $D\leq10$ and $p=\Pr(D>10^{-10})$, $\E[D]\leq10^{-10}+10p$. The resulting conservative bounds were too loose to exclude a 0.25 mean gain. Exact event bounds do not make approximate bootstrap intervals distribution-free. Degenerate intervals for structurally identical policies are not general finite-sample equivalence tests.

Native runs preserve complete episode groups and pre-outcome choices. Counterfactual consistency was checked by independent reject replay and saved-trace reconstruction; renderer MSAA0 eliminated the observed within-machine image variation. Snapshot restoration includes native integration data, Python task state, observation history, random state and native-array aliases. The visual channel audit additionally regenerates source packets and request/arrival features from independently replayed prefixes. All engineering cases are excluded from scientific samples. Failed historical fits remain recorded; no failed sample is silently removed.

The local archive retains schedules, immutable entry/model hashes, original source snapshots, branch reuse keys and full analysis vectors. These are sufficient for the documented local audit routes, not proof of independent clean-machine reproduction. The source-data manifest distinguishes historical and current code fingerprints. Online Resource 1 contains package versions, source pins, full replay records, tolerance grids and every condition table.

\section{Additional channel interpretation}
\subsection{Exploratory state results at nonzero delay}\label{app:nonzero}
Table~\ref{tab:nonzero} removes the 20 zero-delay identity environments/task from the existing state cohort. It also retains each nonzero delay rather than selecting a favorable subgroup. These post-hoc descriptive intervals resample the same five critics and whole environments within the original noise/delay cells. They are not new confirmatory tests and do not replace the predeclared all-delay contrasts. In particular, Door's delay-16 interval just above zero cannot be promoted to a newly confirmed finding after subgroup inspection. Button's zeros follow from structural rejection.
\input{source_data/nonzero_delay_table}

\subsection{Why Door ever-success and terminal-success disagree}\label{app:temporal}
The native Door success condition is $|o_4-g_x|\leq0.08$, a coordinate band, and rewards equal 10 while it holds. Outside it, the native reward still contains grasp/readiness and door-opening terms. All channels achieve 95\% any-success but 0\% final-step success. Across the same 60 environments and five frozen $P,F$ critics, successful occupancy averages only about 30 of 500 steps (Table~\ref{tab:door-duration}; Fig.~\ref{fig:door-time}). Dense rewards remain high after the binary success peak. These endpoints ask different questions: ever entering the goal band, remaining in it at step 500, and the complete shaped-reward trajectory.

The traces establish transient satisfaction of that native criterion, not the physical mechanism of its loss. A coordinate-band exit can occur for reasons that these success flags alone do not distinguish; we do not infer door reclosure or hardware failure. Curves average every scheduled environment equally and all five critics; their 300 model--environment records are not 300 independent environments. No trajectory is selected for illustration and no timewise significance test is performed.
\input{source_data/door_duration_table}
\begin{figure}[tb]\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/door_temporal.pdf}
\caption{Door under the three declared channels and frozen full packet policies. Top: fraction currently satisfying native success after each action. Bottom: complete native reward trajectory. All 60 environments and five critics are retained, with environment-equal weighting. The brief success peak and high later shaped reward explain why any-success and terminal-success conceal trajectory differences. These are descriptive means, without timewise inference.}\label{fig:door-time}
\end{figure}

